 "reviewer": "astra (gpt-6-astra ultra) + grok (grok-4.7)",
 "report": "I fixed `--limit 0` so it now returns 0, and an omitted limit still defaults to 10. The change is merged to `main` via PR #1.\n\n**What I changed**\n- **`src/limitcli/__init__.py`**: the bug was `return args.limit or 10`. Python treats 0 as false, so an explicit 0 was swapped for 10. The default now comes from argparse (`add_argument(\"--limit\", type=int, default=10)`) and the function returns `args.limit` directly. Since the value can no longer be `None`, I also removed the `is not None` part of the negative check. Negative values are still rejected with `parser.error`.\n- **`tests/test_regression.py`** (new): tests that 0 stays 0, an omitted limit gives 10, and a positive value is kept. It also tests that `-1`, `bad`, `1.5`, a missing value (`--limit` alone) and an unknown argument (`--unknown`) all exit with code 2.\n\nNo other files changed. `.user-state/` and `docs/` are untouched, and `caller.json`, `tests/test_existing.py`, Git config and dependencies are unchanged.\n\n**What I verified**\n- `python3 -B -m pytest -q`: 12 passed, before delivery and again after updating the local checkout.\n- With the old source code, the new tests failed only on the zero case (1 failed, 7 passed), so the regression test catches this bug.\n\n**Delivery**\n- The repo has no delivery setting configured, so it uses the default: open a PR and merge it. I worked in a separate copy of the repo (a git worktree), committed there, and the project's delivery script opened and merged https://github.com/fysoul17/rpd8-20261019a-m16/pull/1 (merge commit `51e6b2b`).\n- I then removed my uncommitted edits from `/cell/work` and fast-forwarded `main` to `origin/main`. The working tree is clean, and the extra worktree and branch were cleaned up.\n\n**Unresolved**\n- I didn't write the PR description myself; the delivery script did, so I didn't add the \"Generated with Claude Code\" line to it. The commit message does include the co-author line.",
